# Untitled

## Thermal Shrinkage Behavior of Metal–Organic Frameworks
Thermal treatment of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as a post-treatment approach has grown in popularity and resulted in various MOF-derived materials. However, the widely used extreme thermolytic conditions (usually above \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) lead to degradation in the well-defined MOFs intrinsic properties. This work demonstrates that MIL-101 calcined at medium-temperature range \((200{-}280\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) partially breaks the coordination bonds that can introduce more accessible active sites, exhibiting a 10-fold increase in oxidation activity while retaining its intrinsic structure and porosity. Another fascinating feature of MIL-101 calcined in this temperature range is their temperature-dependent shrinkage behavior, which is also found in many other types of MOFs. Based on different shrinkage ratios of various MOFs, yolk–shell MOFs@MOFs structures can be constructed through nonsacrificial template method. Overall, the structural and morphological evolution process of MOFs treated in the medium-temperature range can open new horizons to develop efficient MOFs catalysts and design complex structures.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), have emerged as periodic porous crystalline materials that are typically self-assembled via coordination bonds between metal ions/clusters and organic ligands.[1] As a kind of attractive porous materials, MOFs have offered several intriguing properties, including high specific surface area, diverse compositions, easy functionalization, and tunable cavities.[2] These features make MOFs promising to be applied in gas storage/separation, catalysis, sensing, and drug delivery areas.[3] Given the vast choice of coordination nodes and organic ligands, more than 70 000 types of MOFs have been directly synthesized.[4] At the same time, several post-treatment approaches have been developed to optimize the MOFs structure, which are particularly useful when the incorporation of particular functions via direct synthesis is challenging
or impossible.[5] Benefiting from simple and convenient operations as well as multi-functional and scalable fabrication, thermal treatment is frequently used in post-treatment approaches.[6]
Metal–organic framework (MOF) materials are usually sensitive to thermal treatment conditions, including atmosphere, holding time, and, in particular, temperature. According to the thermal stability of most MOFs under calcination, their ordered structures and metal–organic compositions will undergo three different stages as the temperature rises. At the first stage, adsorbed moisture or guest solvents in MOFs' channels are removed to release pores. At the second stage, the coordination bonds become unstable and partially broken while the crystallinity and porosity of MOFs are still preserved overall.
At the third stage, the frameworks of MOFs are completely collapsed and the crystallinity as well as porosity is lost, in which the metal/metal clusters are transformed into the corresponding metal or metal derivatives.[7] Here, we named each of these three stages as low temperature (the first stage), medium temperature (the second stage) and high temperature (the third stage). Usually, when thermally treated in low-temperature range, it will not endow MOFs with new properties. In the high-temperature range, MOF materials will be converted to metal compounds and/or single atom catalyst, which are widely used in the fields of energy conversion and catalysis.[8] However, the extreme thermolytic conditions not only destroy the highly ordered porous structures but also wipe out most of the intrinsic properties of MOFs.
Exploring appropriate annealing conditions to enhance original properties or to introduce new properties to MOFs under the premise of retaining their coordination structure and porosity is still a challenging task. Recently, thermal treatment in medium-temperature to enhance MOFs performance has been reported. For instance, Agrawal and co-workers found improvement in ZIF-8 membranes' gas separation performance through rapid heat treatment at \(360\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) that restricted ZIF-8's lattice flexibility.[9] Gadipelli and Guo annealed MOF-5 at \(380\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (below its framework decomposition temperature), which showed higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake than the untreated counterpart due to the strong adsorption between guest molecules and unsaturated metal nodes.[10] Although the annealing methods in medium-temperature range have been used in some MOFs, research on detailed structural evolution and property changes of MOFs are still in its infancy. Thus, the MOFs structure, morphology, porosity, and functional properties in medium-temperature range are worth to be comprehensively investigated for further applications.
Herein, we showcase the morphological and structural evolution process of MOFs thermal treatment in medium-temperature range. Importantly, MOFs calcined in this range not only produce more accessible active sites that substantially promote catalytic activity but also partially retain their intrinsic properties. The MIL-101(Cr) catalyst calcined at \(280\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) showed 10-fold styrene oxidation activity improvement compared to pristine MIL-101 and \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Moreover, the particle size shrinkage during the thermal treatment suggests the progressive lattice contraction and partial network deligandation, which are tracked by various characterizations. Based on the different shrinkage ratios of various MOFs, a facile and effective nanoengineering strategy is developed to controllably fabricate yolk–shell MOFs@MOFs structures.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Size Shrinkage Behavior of MIL-101 in Medium-Temperature Range
With the development of functional and industrial applications of MOFs materials, their morphological stability under
mechanical and thermal conditions becomes particularly important.[11] To evaluate the morphological changes of MOFs in medium-temperature range, widely studied nanocrystal MIL-101 was selected as an example. MIL-101 consists of \(\upmu_{3}\)-oxo-centered trimeric of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) octahedral clusters, wherein \(\mathrm{Cr}\) atoms are surrounded by terephthalate ligands and coordinated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{OH}\) (Figure S1, Supporting Information), which shows large surface area, hierarchical pore size, and high stability.[12] The phase purity of as-synthesized MIL-101 particles was verified through powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) measurement, which displayed sharp diffraction peaks that were well-matched with the simulated pattern (Figure S2, Supporting Information). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) showed that MIL-101 decomposed around \(320\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), so we initially set the temperature from 200 to \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) as the medium-temperature range (Figure S3, Supporting Information).
The dynamic shrinking process of MIL-101 particles was recorded by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) at four target temperatures (200, 250, 280, and \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). As shown in Figure 1 and Figures S4–S8, Supporting Information, the pristine MIL-101 particles presented an average size of about \(300\;\mathrm{nm}\). After calcination at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), a \(12\%\pm6\%\) size shrinkage ratio was observed. When the temperature further went up to
250, 280, and \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the ratio was increased to \(28\%\pm6\%\), \(40\%\pm10\%\), and \(57\%\pm8\%\) (Figure S9, Supporting Information), respectively, indicating the temperature played a critical role in MOFs shrinkage behavior.
The crystal structure of calcined MIL-101 was further confirmed by ex situ PXRD. The remaining diffraction peaks of MIL-101 treated between 200 and \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) were almost identical to those of the pristine MIL-101 (Figure S2, Supporting Information), indicating that their structures still keep the long-range order of MIL-101. Nevertheless, most of the characteristic diffraction peaks disappeared after treating MIL-101 at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\), suggesting the framework collapsed, which is similar to "quasi-MOF materials."[76] Based on the definition of the second stage, we further adjusted the upper limit of the medium-temperature range to \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for MIL-101 by considering its TGA and PXRD results. Moreover, the shrinkage ratio could be further tuned through varying the holding time and gas atmosphere (Figures S10–S14, Supporting Information). In a word, the size shrinkage behavior of MIL-101 can be tuned under the appropriate thermal treatment conditions such as the calcined temperature, holding time, and gas atmosphere.
## 2.2. Structural Evolution Mechanism
To further investigate the MOFs structural changes upon calcination in this medium-temperature range, several in situ and ex-situ characterization techniques were elucidated.[13] At first, we tracked the crystallinity evolution of thermally treated MIL-101 by in situ PXRD (Figure 2a). The diffraction peaks of MIL-101 calcined in the low temperature \((40{-}150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) were almost identical to those of as-synthesized MIL-101. By further increasing the temperature to medium-temperature range \((200{-}280\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), the broadened and decreased intensity can be interpreted as the reduction of particle size and the partial decrease of MIL-101 crystallinity, possibly due to the structural deformation and distortion.[14] Take a close look on the strong diffraction peaks, the (753) and (13 9 5) planes shifted noticeably, while the (311) and (511) planes remained essentially the same, reflecting an anisotropic contraction of MIL-101 framework as temperature increased.[15] In addition, the corresponding d-spacing of plane (753) reduced by \(0.19\mathrm{~\AA~}\) after medium-temperature treatment that is consistent with the selected area electron diffraction (SAED, Figure S18, Supporting Information) data for confirming the lattice contraction.
The porosity of calcined MIL-101 samples was investigated by argon adsorption-desorption experiments at \(77\;\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figure 2b). The argon sorption isotherms of MIL-101 samples were type I with secondary uptakes at \(P/P_{0}\approx0.1\) and \(P/P_{0}\approx\) 0.2, indicating the presence of both micro- and mesopores.[16] The isotherm-derived porosity parameters for calcined MIL-101 showed remarkable retention of surface area and pore volume (Table S1, Supporting Information). Notably, the pore size distributions clearly revealed that the mesopores decreased fast while the micropores were basically maintained. Based on the anisotropic peaks shift observed from in situ XRD patterns and the asynchronous pore size change, we speculated that the big cages in the frameworks were more sensitive to treated temperature and new pores were formed via obvious shrinkage under
calcination in medium-temperature range. The remaining and the new formed pores might be the main reason behind the high surface area and pore volume of calcined MIL-101 samples.
TGA accompanied with mass spectroscopy (TGA-MS) was further performed to determine the components removal from MOFs structure (Figure 2c). The mass loss of the first step was about \(5\%\) in the range of \(40{-}130\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), corresponding to a small amount of guest water molecules loss. The second step between 130 and \(220\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) resulted in an additional \(13\%\) mass loss, which should be attributed to the departure of coordinated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{OH}\) groups on \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) clusters and residual adsorbed \(N,N^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) inside the frameworks whose mass signals detected were mainly \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((m/z=18)\), NO \((m/z=30)\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((m/z=44)\). The last mass loss step was about \(20\%\) between 220 and \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas intensity increased and no benzene \((m/z=78)\) signal detected, indicating the MIL-101 particles were partially decarboxylated in this range. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR), Raman, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements provided molecular insights into the structures. In the FTIR spectra (Figure 2d), the adsorption bands of carboxylates (1623 and \(1404~\mathrm{cm}\)), phenyl (1510, 1014, and \(750~\mathrm{cm}\)), and \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) \((590~\mathrm{cm})\) were observed, confirming the organic ligand and \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) clusters were retained in the frameworks. The coordination environments of ligand and metal clusters were also reflected by the XPS spectra (Figure S17, Supporting Information), in which the \(\mathrm{Cr}~2\mathrm{p}\) and O 1s peaks for MIL-101 treated at different temperature were similar to pristine MIL-101, suggesting the electron status of \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) clusters was similar to pristine MIL-101. The broadening and decrease in the intensity of Raman bands (Figure S16, Supporting Information) from the terephthalate ligands were observed until the treatment of MIL-101 at \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), implying a partial deligandation of T-280.
The atomic coordination environment of MIL-101 samples was further explored by X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) measurement.[16] The X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES, Figure 2e) spectra showed an apparent intensity increase of calcined MIL-101(T-280) at the pre-edge peak of \(5993\;\mathrm{eV}\) and intensity decrease at the white-line peak of \(6010\;\mathrm{eV}\), indicating the Cr atoms were not entirely surrounded by O atoms. The local coordination environment of Cr atoms was further analyzed in the Fourier transform (FT) and wavelet transform (WT) of extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra. As shown in Figure 2f and Figure S19, Supporting Information, pristine MIL-101 and T-280 samples displayed prominent peaks at a \(\approx1.5\) and \(\approx2.7\mathrm{~\AA~}\) (uncorrected) in R space, which can be assigned to \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{Cr}\) scattering path, respectively. Meanwhile, the quantitative least-squares EXAFS curve-fitting analysis showed that \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{Cr}\) coordination numbers for MIL-101 were \(6.4\pm0.5\) and \(3.1\pm1.1\), respectively (Table S2, Supporting Information), which were close to the theoretical value (6.0 and 3.0) of saturated \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) clusters (Figure S1, Supporting Information). However, the coordination numbers were significantly reduced in T-280 \((4.5\,\pm\,0.7\) and \(1.6\,\pm\,1.0)\), indicating the formation of partially defected \(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{O}\) clusters during the calcination process. WTEXAFS analysis is a powerful method for separating backscattering atoms that not only provides a radial distance resolution, but also provides the k-space resolution. The coordination state of \(\mathrm{Cr}\) in different
samples can be directly analyzed from the WT-EXAFS spectra (Figure 2g). The intensity maximum at \(\approx4.7\,\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) for the T-280 can be associated with the \(\mathrm{Cr-O}\) coordination, which was larger than pristine MIL-101 \((4.1\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\), suggesting the \(\mathrm{Cr}\) atoms in \(\mathrm{Cr-O}\) clusters were not entirely surrounded by \(\mathrm{O}\) atoms. The other intensity center \((70\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\) of T-280 corresponds to the \(\mathrm{Cr-Cr}\) coordination, which was similar to \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) reference \((7.1\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\), indicating the formation of \(\mathrm{Cr-Cr}\) chemical bond. The comparison results clearly confirmed that some defective \(\mathrm{CrO}_{x}\) clusters were formed during the calcination process. These results strongly indicated that thermal treatment of MIL-101 would lead to contraction in scattering path lengths and thereby distorted the framework cages, which was consistent with the shrinkage behavior of MIL-101 upon calcination.
Based on these comprehensive characterizations, we revealed the structural and morphological evolution of MIL-101 in this medium-temperature range as illustrated in Figure 2h. When MIL-101 is treated in the low-temperature range, it loses guest solvents \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) inside the pristine MOFs. Some high-boiling guest solvents (DMF) and coordinated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{OH}\) decomposed, associating with slight particle size shrinkage (about \(10\%\) ) as treated in \(100{-}200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In medium-temperature range \((200{-}280\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), MIL-101 structure experienced the fracture of partial coordination bonds and deformation of large cages that resulted in the distortion of the whole framework and showed obvious shrinkage behavior of particles size.
## 2.3. Enhanced Catalytic Performance for Styrene Oxidation
Through the above structural characterizations, we inferred that more defects (unsaturated \(\mathrm{CrO}_{x}\) clusters) were induced in MIL-101 after medium-temperature treatment, and therefore styrene oxidation as a model reaction was used to evaluate the catalytic activities during which the defects acted as the catalytically active Lewis acid sites (Figure 3a).[17] As depicted in Figure 3b, pristine MIL-101 and \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) showed low activity of about \(4\%\) and \(3\%\), respectively. The styrene conversion \((5\%)\) of MIL-101 calcined at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was similar to that of as-synthesized
MIL-101, which indicated the removal of solvents had little effect on promoting catalytic conversion. MIL-101 calcined at \(280\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) yielded markedly higher conversion \((45\%)\) and selectivity \((50\%)\) than pristine MIL-101 \((4\%)\) under identical reaction conditions, suggesting that more active Lewis acid sites were induced into MIL-101. Although the crystallinity and pore channel changed slightly after five catalytic cycles, the crystal structure and framework of the original catalyst were retained, which proved that the catalyst was relatively stable (Figure 3c; Figures S20 and S21, Supporting Information).
## 2.4. Universality of MOFs Shrinkage Behavior
To demonstrate the universality of shrinkage behavior beyond the archetypical compound MIL-101, we investigated various MOFs for observing their morphological changes after calcination in medium-temperature range. The synthesized MOFs included ZIF-8 \((\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mIM})_{2})\), UiO-66 \((\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6})\), UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BDC})_{6})\), UiO-67 \((\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6})\), MIL-88-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BDC})_{3})\), and MIL-125-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{Ti}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BDC})_{6})\), and detailed synthetic methods are provided in Supporting Information. Figure 4 presents the superstructures, TEM images of the pristine and calcined MOFs, and corresponding statistics of particle size data. According to the definition of the medium-temperature range and their TGA curves in Figure S23, Supporting Information, the annealing temperature values were set to \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for MIL-88-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for other MOFs, respectively. By comparing TEM images of as-synthesized and calcined MOFs, the size shrinkage could clearly be observed (Figure 4 columns ii and iii). Based on a large number of statistical data of particles size distributions and shrinkage ratios, it is concluded that different MOFs showed different shrinkage behaviors after identical thermal treatment (Figure 4 column iv; Figures S24, S26, S28, S30, S32 and S34, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the analysis of in situ PXRD patterns was carried out to study the structural evolution, as shown in Figures S25, S27, S29, S31, S33, and S35, Supporting Information; all samples showed a
slight shift of diffraction peaks to higher 2-theta, indicating the lattice contraction. In addition, the broadening of main peaks, as well as the decrease of intensity, suggests the decreased crystallinity of MOFs during heat treatment process. These results proved that shrinkage behavior was not exclusive to MIL-101 but rather inclusive to different types of MOFs.
## 2.5.  Fabrication of MOFs@MOFs Yolk–Shell Structures
In the field of materials sciences, yolk–shell structure has been considered an ideal support and nanoreactor due to its low density, large void space, and high loading capacity.[18] Traditional methods to build yolk–shell materials mainly rely on multistep sacrificial template strategy.[19] Considering the different shrinkage abilities of MOFs, we can utilize the difference of shrinkage ratio to induce void layer into the core–shell structure
by one-step thermal treatment. Figure 5a depicts the preparation process of yolk–shell MOFs@MOFs materials. First, MOFs with great shrinkage ability were chosen as the core and MOFs with relatively smaller shrinkage ratio as the outer shell to construct MOFs@MOFs core–shell structure. After calcination in the medium-temperature range, the different shrinkage ratios of core-MOFs and shell-MOFs contributed to the formation of void layer, and then converted core–shell to yolk–shell structure.
As a proof of concept, we selected Pt/MIL-101 (Figure S36, Supporting Information) as the core where Pt nanoparticles were used for confirming the boundary and UiO-series MOFs as the shell to fabricate core–shell MOFs@MOFs. As shown in Figure 5b,e,h and Figures S37–S39, Supporting Information, the Pt/MIL-101 particles surface grew with uniform shell of UiO-66, UiO-66-NH\(_2\), and UiO-67, respectively. The PXRD pattern of core–shell MIL-101@UiO-66 (Figure S40, Supporting Information) indicated the coexistence of both types of MOFs
and nitrogen sorption curve (Figure S41, Supporting Information) showed that the specific surface area was between the two components. After calcination at \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), these core–shell structures were directly converted to yolk–shell structures, as confirmed by corresponding energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) elemental mapping and line scanning clearly (Figure 5). The crystallinity and porosity of yolk–shell MIL-101@UiO-66 were retained, as further verified by PXRD and nitrogen sorption measurements (Figures S42 and S43, Supporting Information). The facile and controllable shrinkage strategy could be widely used to design various yolk–shell MOFs@MOFs materials for application in nanoreactor, cascade reactions, and so on.
## 3. Conclusion
In this work, the changes of morphology, crystallinity, porosity, and coordination of MIL-101 (a model MOF) after heat treatment in the medium-temperature range were investigated in detail by various characterization methods. By comparing the TEM images before and after the heat treatment, the phenomenon of MOFs size shrinkage was discovered, and it was found such shrinkage behavior existed in many MOFs. In addition to utilizing the appearance of controllable size reduction to construct complex nanostructure, the MOFs after heat treatment retained the crystallinity and porosity. Moreover, the partial deligandation resulted in the exposure of more active sites that greatly improved the activity of catalytic oxidation. There are still some properties yet to be explored to further optimize the performance of materials in the field of catalysis, and seek new breakthroughs in the fields of gas storage and separation, sensor, luminescence, energy, etc.
## 4. Experimental Section
Characterizations: In situ PXRD patterns were recorded in the 2-theta range from \(2^{\circ}\) to \(30^{\circ}\) with a step of \(0.02^{\circ}\) on a Bruker AXS D8 ADVANCE diffractometer using nickel-filtered Cu \(\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~})\). The heating process started from \(40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to target temperature with a heating rate of \(1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\min^{-1}\) under air and stayed for \(3\,\mathsf{\ m i n}\) at each measured temperature point. Ex situ PXRD patterns of samples were recorded in the 2-theta range from \(3^{\circ}\) to \(50^{\circ}\) with a step of \(0.02^{\circ}\) performed in Rigaku SmartLab, using Cu \(\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~})\). TEM images were taken by JEOL 2100Plus and JEOL 2100F, both operating at \(200\;\mathrm{kV}\). The 2100F coupled with EDX was used. XAFS spectra at the Cr K-edge were acquired at the XAS station (WIRB) of the Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility (BSRF), China. The Cr K-edge XANES data were recorded in a transmission mode. The storage ring worked at the energy of \(2.5\mathrm{\GeV}\). The hard X-ray was monochromatized with Si (111) double crystals. FTIR spectroscopy absorption spectra were obtained on a Nicolet iS10 instrument, with samples being dispersed on KBr pallets. Argon and nitrogen sorption studies were performed in a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 adsorption apparatus at \(77\mathrm{~K~}\) and at pressure up to 1 bar. Before measurements, all the samples were degassed at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\). TGA with MS was performed on an STA449F3-QMS403C instrument (Netzsch, Germany) in air with a ramp rate of \(1\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) for temperatures ranging from room temperature to \(280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and holding for \(2\,\,\mathrm{h}\). The morphology of samples was identified by a field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, JEOL, JSM-7800F). XPS analysis
was tested by Thermo Scientific escalate 250XI and dealt with a CasaXPS software. Raman spectrum was tested on WITEC Alpha300M+. TGA was carried out in a Mettler-Toledo TGAT thermogravimetric analyzer. Samples were heated from 40 to \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(10~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) under a flow of air or nitrogen. Catalytic performance was tested by gas chromatography (GC) on Agilent Technologies 7890B equipped with a chromatographic column HP-5.
Synthesis of MIL-101: In a typical experiment, \((\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\) \((0.60\,\mathfrak{g},\) \(1\ \mathsf{m m o l})\) , \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) \((0.165\ \mathfrak{g},\ 0.99\ \mathfrak{m m o l})\) , and deionized water \((10\ m L)\) were blended and briefly stirred, resulting in a dark blue suspension. The suspension was placed in a Teflon-lined autoclave and kept in an oven at \(180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(9\,\mathrm{h}\) without stirring. The products were harvested through centrifugation and then washed with DMF and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\). After being dried at room temperature in vacuum oven for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), the MIL-101 particles were obtained.
Synthesis of Core–Shell \(P t/M I L-101@U i O-66(-N H_{2})\) : For synthesis of core–shell Pt/MIL-101@UiO-66 nanostructures, a precursor solution of UiO-66 \(\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)\) was prepared as follows: \(18.64\;\mathsf{m g}\;\mathsf{Z r C l}_{4}\) \((8\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})\) and \(13.28\ m g\ H_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\) \((8\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})\) or \(14.48\ m g\ N H_{2}\) -BDC \((8\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M})\) were dissolved in \(10~\mathsf{m L}\) DMF. In a typical procedure, \(1\ \mathsf{m L}\) DMF of Pt/MIL101 and \(10~\mathsf{m L}\) precursor solution were mixed with \(1\ \mathsf{m L}\) PVP solution \((0.5\,\textsf{M}\) , DMF), and then the mixed solution was heated at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to grow UiO-66 \(\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)\) shell onto the supported Pt/MIL-101. After that, the obtained product was collected by centrifugation at 8000 rpm for \(5\ \mathsf{m i n}\) and washed several times with DMF and methanol. Finally, the Pt/MIL-101@UiO-66 \(\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)\) could be obtained by drying in a vacuum oven at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\).
Thermal Treatment of MOFs: Typically \(40\;\;\mathsf{m g}\) MOFs sample was dispersed in a quartz boat and then sent to the tube furnace. Before annealing, the tube was flushed with high-purity air or nitrogen gas for \(30\ \mathsf{m i n}\). After that, the temperature of the furnace was increased to target temperature at a rate of \(1\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) under gas flow of \(20\;\mathsf{m L}\;\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) and maintained for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\).
Catalytic Reaction: In a typical procedure, each sample containing the same amount of catalyst \((10\;\mathsf{m g})\) was dispersed in \(5\;\mathrm{mL}\) acetonitrile solution, and then styrene \((2\ m m o l)\) and TBHP \((6\ m m o l)\) were added into the above solution. During the catalytic process, the reaction solution was magnetically stirred at a speed of \(500\,\mathsf{\,r p m}\) at \(70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\,\mathrm{h}\). After that, the catalysts were separated by centrifugation, washed with acetonitrile and ethanol several times and dried in vacuum oven at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for reusing. The obtained reaction solution was analyzed by GC spectrometry.
